WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.009
A payment record in Redis is a hash: three fields in one key.

2
00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:09.199
That's 35 bytes of actual data.

3
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:18.333
Stored like Java's HashMap, that's slots, an entry per field, and a separate string for each field and value, joined by 8 byte pointers.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.880
On the lab server that took 215 bytes, mostly bookkeeping.

5
00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:30.992
A listpack drops the structure: the entries go one after another into one block, each with its length in front.

6
00:00:30.992 --> 00:00:31.635
77 bytes.

7
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:35.891
HGET walks the block from the start, skipping ahead by each length.

8
00:00:35.891 --> 00:00:38.173
A few short entries are quick to scan.

9
00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:44.843
Walking 10,000 fields that way, on the one thread every client shares, is too slow.

10
00:00:44.843 --> 00:00:50.305
So past 512 fields, or a value over 64 bytes, Redis converts the hash.

11
00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:56.662
Now it's a hash table: the field's hash picks its slot, so a lookup costs the same at any size.

12
00:00:56.662 --> 00:00:58.032
TYPE still says hash.

13
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.075
A sorted set needs a member's score fast, and members in score order.

14
00:01:03.075 --> 00:01:09.221
Past 128 members, Redis keeps both: a hash table from member to score, and a skip list.

15
00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:12.723
A skip list is a sorted list with express links.

16
00:01:12.723 --> 00:01:19.087
A search for 40 rides the top lane, and drops down before it overshoots: about log N steps.

17
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:26.301
The switch costs memory: about one kilobyte at 128 members, nearly eleven at 129.

18
00:01:26.301 --> 00:01:28.645
And it never converts back.

19
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.716
Big keys hurt in another way.

20
00:01:30.716 --> 00:01:34.019
A set of flagged cards grows to a million members.

21
00:01:34.019 --> 00:01:41.023
SMEMBERS reads it whole, holding the only thread for about 54 milliseconds while every other client waits.

22
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:50.188
Read it in pages with SSCAN, so other clients get turns in between, and delete it with UNLINK, which frees the memory on a background thread.

23
00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:52.860
Small keys stay compact and get scanned.

24
00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:58.657
Big ones get real structures, and a command that touches every element still blocks everyone.
